course: dictionary
lesson: 11301
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. You can tell which meaning an author means by reading the words around it. The word yarn is a good example. In «Knitting machines have hundreds of tiny hooked needles that pull loops of yarn through other loops», yarn is thread. In «spin a yarn», yarn means a story.
explain: Look at the clues in «Knitting machines have hundreds of tiny hooked needles that pull loops of yarn through other loops.» Needles and loops tell you that yarn is the thread you knit with, so it must mean thread here.
explain: Now read «You've been listening to some of the yarns they spin in the public-houses about here.» The word spin is a clue. People spin stories, not thread, so yarns here are long tales. Always ask: which meaning fits this sentence? Try it in the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: In Australia and New Zealand, people use yarn in a friendly way too. To have a yarn means to have a chat. When the clues are not enough, a dictionary will settle it.
fact: Yarn is a long thread made of wool, cotton or other fibre, used for knitting, crocheting and weaving.
fact: «Yarn» can also mean a long story, often exciting or not quite true.
fact: To «spin a yarn» means to tell an invented story.
fact: In Australia and New Zealand, «have a yarn» means to have a friendly chat.
fact: Yarn has a plural, yarns, and it can also be a verb: to yarn means to talk for a while in a friendly way.
exercise: «Knitting machines have hundreds of tiny hooked needles that pull loops of yarn through other loops.» What is yarn in this sentence? | A long, made-up story | *A long thread for knitting | A friendly chat | A ball of paper
exercise: «You've been listening to some of the yarns they spin in the public-houses about here.» What are yarns in this sentence? | Petrol stations | Balls of wool for knitting | Sharp needles | *Long tales, which may not be true
exercise: Someone in Australia says, «Let's have a yarn.» What does that mean? | *Let's have a friendly chat | Let's knit a scarf together | Let's tell a made-up tale only | Let's buy some wool
test: «yarn knitted into scarves» What is yarn in this phrase? | A kind of paper | *Thread made of wool or cotton | A made-up story | A type of glue
test: What does «spin a yarn» mean? | To have a friendly chat in Australia | To knit a very long scarf | *To tell an invented story | To spin wool on a wheel
exam: Which meaning of yarn fits this sentence: «You've been listening to some of the yarns they spin in the public-houses about here.»? | A friendly chat, used only in Australia | Thread for knitting | Loops made by a knitting machine | *Long tales, which may not be true
